cutable Agentic Memory (EAM), a structured Knowledge Graph that shifts GUI planning from free-form generation to robust retrieval-and-execution process. EAM outperforms UI-TARS-7B by up to 19.6% on AndroidWorld while reducing token costs 6x relative to GPT-4o, with 2.8s average latency.\",\n    25→    \"author\": \"Zerui Qin, Sheng Yue, Xingyuan Hua, Yongjian Fu, Ju Ren\",\n    26→    \"source\": \"Paper\",\n    27→    \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.12294\",\n    28→    \"published_date\": \"2026-05-12\",\n    29→    \"topics\": [\"GUI agents\", \"knowledge graphs\", \"memory systems\", \"MCTS\", \"Android automation\"],\n    30→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n    31→  },\n    32→  {\n    33→    \"title\": \"ALGOGEN: Tool-Generated Verifiable Traces for Reliable Algorithm Visualization\",\n    34→    \"description\": \"Proposes a novel paradigm that decouples algorithm execution from rendering using Visualization Trace Algebra (VTA). LLM generates Python tracker that outputs VTA-JSON traces, while deterministic renderer compiles traces with RSL into outputs. Achieves 99.8% success rate vs 82.5% for end-to-end methods on LeetCode AV benchmark.\",\n    35→    \"author\": \"Kunpeng Liao, Yuexiao Ma, Yisheng Lin, Hualin Zeng, Xiawu Zheng, Rongrong Ji\",\n    36→    \"source\": \"Paper\",\n    37→    \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.12159\",\n    38→    \"published_date\": \"2026-05-12\",\n    39→    \"topics\": [\"algorithm visualization\", \"LLM hallucination\", \"code generation\", \"educational tools\", \"rendering\"],\n    40→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n    41→  },\n    42→  {\n    43→    \"title\": \"Intermediate Artifacts as First-Class Citizens: A Data Model for Durable Intermediate Artifacts in Agentic Systems\",\n    44→    \"description\": \"Argues that AI systems should preserve durable, inspectable intermediate artifacts (typed, structured, addressable, versioned, dependency-aware) rather than just final outputs. These artifacts make AI-generated work more inspectable, revisable, and maintainable over time for multi-step AI workflows.\",\n    45→    \"author\": \"Josh Rosen, Seth Rosen\",\n    46→    \"source\": \"Paper\",\n    47→    \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.12087\",\n    48→    \"published_date\": \"2026-05-12\",\n    49→    \"topics\": [\"agentic systems\", \"data modeling\", \"intermediate artifacts\", \"AI workflows\", \"system design\"],\n    50→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n    51→  },\n    52→  {\n    53→    \"title\": \"OpenAI's WebRTC Problem for Voice AI\",\n    54→    \"description\": \"Luke Curley identifies a critical issue with OpenAI's voice AI implementation: WebRTC is designed to degrade audio quality during poor network conditions to maintain low latency, but this is problematic for LLM voice interactions. When audio packets are dropped, the prompt becomes garbage and produces garbage responses. Users would rather wait 200ms for accurate prompts than have distorted audio. The fundamental problem: WebRTC implementation is hard-coded for real-time latency and makes it impossible to retransmit audio packets within a browser. This highlights the tension between traditional VoIP optimization and LLM voice requirements.\",\n    55→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison (quoting Luke Curley)\",\n    56→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    57→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/May/9/webrtc/\",\n    58→    \"published_date\": \"2026-05-09\",\n    59→    \"topics\": [\"webrtc\", \"openai\", \"voice-ai\", \"latency\", \"llms\"],\n    60→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n    61→  },\n    62→  {\n    63→    \"title\": \"Using Claude Code: The Unreasonable Effectiveness of HTML\",\n    64→    \"description\": \"Thariq Shihipar from Anthropic's Claude Code team advocates for HTML over Markdown as an output format for LLMs. The article demonstrates how requesting HTML output enables rich interactive explanations with SVG diagrams, interactive widgets, and in-page navigation. Simon experiments with us