"https://aider.chat/HISTORY.html\">release notes</a>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class=\"item\">\n                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n                        <a href=\"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/whats-new\">Claude Code: Plugins from .zip archives and URLs</a>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n                        v2.1.128–v2.1.136 (May 4–8, 2026) adds support for loading plugins from .zip archives and URLs via --plugin-dir and --plugin-url. Also includes worktree.baseRef configuration, auto mode hard deny rules, and hooks for effort level tracking.\n                    </div>\n                    <div class=\"item-meta\">\n                        <span class=\"update-type\">feature</span>\n                        May 8, 2026 · <a href=\"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/whats-new\">what's new</a>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class=\"item\">\n                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n                        <a href=\"https://github.com/google-gemini/gemini-cli/releases\">Gemini CLI v0.42.0: Voice Mode, Auto Memory</a>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n                        Nightly builds (May 5-7, 2026) add real-time voice mode with cloud and local backends, Auto Memory inbox flow, Gemma 4 support, ignoreLocalEnv setting, shell command safety evals, LaTeX rendering in TUI, workspace trust enforcement, /bug-memory command, and numerous bug fixes.\n                    </div>\n                    <div class=\"item-meta\">\n                        <span class=\"update-type\">feature</span>\n                        May 7, 2026 · <a href=\"https://github.com/google-gemini/gemini-cli/releases\">releases</a>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class=\"item\">\n                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n                        <a href=\"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/whats-new\">Claude Code: Windows without Git Bash</a>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n                        v2.1.120–v2.1.126 (April 27 – May 1, 2026) removes Git Bash requirement on Windows, using PowerShell instead. Adds 'claude ultrareview' cloud code review, 'claude project purge', and PR URL resume in /resume command.\n                    </div>\n                    <div class=\"item-meta\">\n                        <span class=\"update-type\">feature</span>\n                        May 1, 2026 · <a href=\"https://code.claude.com/docs/en/whats-new\">what's new</a>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class=\"item\">\n                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n                        <a href=\"https://github.blog/changelog/2026-04-30-github-copilot-in-visual-studio-april-update/\">GitHub Copilot: Agentic Workflows in Visual Studio</a>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n                        April 2026 update centers on agentic workflows: cloud agent sessions launch directly from IDE, custom agents gain user-level support, new Debugger agent validates fixes against live runtime, agent skills discovery, customizable keyboard shortcuts, new chat history panel, C++ tools generally available, and auto-decoding in text visualizer.\n                    </div>\n                    <div class=\"item-meta\">\n                        <span class=\"update-type\">feature</span>\n                        April 30, 2026 · <a href=\"https://github.blog/changelog/2026-04-30-github-copilot-in-visual-studio-april-update/\">changelog</a>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n            </section>\n\n            <!-- Technical Insights Section -->\n            <section class=\"column\" id=\"insights\">\n                <div class=\"section-header\">Technical Insights</div>\n\n                <div class=\"item\">\n                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n                        <span class=\"source-badge source-paper\">Paper</span>\n                        <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.12481\">ToolCUA: Towards Optimal GUI-Tool Path Orchestration for Computer Use Agents</a>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n                        Computer Use Agents (CUAs) can act through both atomic GUI actions and high-level tool calls, but this hybrid action space often leads to suboptimal execution paths. ToolCUA proposes an end-to-end agent designed to learn optimal GUI-Tool path selection through a staged training paradigm.\n                    </div>\n                    <div class=\"item-meta\">\n                        <span style=\"color: #828282;\">Xuhao Hu et al.</span> · May 12, 2026 · <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.12481\">arXiv</a>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class=\"topics\">\n                        <span class=\"tag\">GUI agents</span>\n                        <span class=\"tag\">tool orchestration</span>\n                        <span class=\"tag\">computer use agents</span>\n                        <span class=\"tag\">LLM agents</span>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class=\"item\">\n                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n                        <span class=\"source-badge source-paper\">Paper</span>\n                        <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.11946\">Counterfactual Trace Auditing of LLM Agent Skills</a>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n                        Introduces Counterfactual Trace Auditing (CTA), a framework for measuring how skills change LLM agent behavior. Testing on SWE-Skills-Bench with Claude across 49 software engineering tasks revealed that skills substantially reshape agent behavior even when pass rates remain nearly unchanged.\n                    </div>\n                    <div class=\"item-meta\">\n                        <span style=\"color: #828282;\">Xiaolin Zhou et al.</span> · May 12, 2026 · <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.11946\">arXiv</a>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class=\"topics\">\n                        <span class=\"tag\">LLM agent evaluation</span>\n                        <span class=\"tag\">skill auditing</span>\n                        <span class=\"tag\">benchmarking</span>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class=\"item\">\n                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n                        <span class=\"source-badge source-paper\">Paper</span>\n                        <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.12294\">Executable Agentic Memory for GUI Agent</a>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n                        Proposes Executable Agentic Memory (EAM), a structured Knowledge Graph that shifts GUI planning from free-form generation to robust retrieval-and-execution process. EAM outperforms UI-TARS-7B by up to 19.6% on AndroidWorld while reducing token costs 6x relative to GPT-4o.\n                    </div>\n                    <div class=\"item-meta\">\n                        <span style=\"color: #828282;\">Zerui Qin et al.</span> · May 12, 2026 · <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.12294\">arXiv</a>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class=\"topics\">\n                        <span class=\"tag\">GUI agents</span>\n                        <span class=\"tag\">knowledge graphs</span>\n                        <span class=\"tag\">memory systems</span>\n                        <span class=\"tag\">MCTS</span>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class=\"item\">\n                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n                        <span class=\"source-badge source-paper\">Paper</span>\n                        <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.12159\">ALGOGEN: Tool-Generated Verifiable Traces for Reliable Algorithm Visualization</a>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n                        Proposes a novel paradigm that decouples algorithm execution from rendering using Visualization Trace Algebra (VTA). LLM generates Python tracker that outputs VTA-JSON traces, while deterministic renderer compiles traces. Achieves 99.8% success rate vs 82.5% for end-to-end methods on LeetCode AV benchmark.\n                    </div>\n                    <div class=\"item-meta\">\n                        <span style=\"color: #828282;\">Kunpeng Liao et al.</span> · May 12, 2026 · <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.12159\">arXiv</a>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class=\"topics\">\n                        <span class=\"tag\">algorithm visualization</span>\n                        <span class=\"tag\">LLM hallucination</span>\n                        <span class=\"tag\">code generation</span>\n                        <span class=\"tag\">educational tools</span>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class=\"item\">\n                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n                        <span class=\"source-badge source-paper\">Paper</span>\n                        <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.12087\">Intermediate Artifacts as First-Class Citizens</a>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n                        Argues that AI systems should preserve durable, inspectable intermediate artifacts (typed, structured, addressable, versioned, dependency-aware) rather than just final outputs. These artifacts make AI-generated work more inspectable, revisable, and maintainable over time for multi-step AI workflows.\n                    </div>\n                    <div class=\"item-meta\">\n                        <span style=\"color: #828282;\">Josh Rosen, Seth Rosen</span> · May 12, 2026 · <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.12087\">arXiv</a>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class=\"topics\">\n                        <span class=\"tag\">agentic systems</span>\n                        <span class=\"tag\">data modeling</span>\n                        <span class=\"tag\">intermediate artifacts</span>\n                        <span class=\"tag\">AI workflows</span>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class=\"item\">\n                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n                        <span class=\"source-badge source-blog\">Blog</span>\n                        <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/May/9/webrtc/\">OpenAI's WebRTC Problem for Voice AI</a>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n                        Luke Curley identifies a critical issue with OpenAI's voice AI implementation: WebRTC is designed to degrade audio quality during poor network conditions to maintain low latency, but this is problematic for LLM voice interactions. When audio packets are dropped, the prompt becomes garbage and produces garbage responses.\n                    </div>\n                    <div class=\"item-meta\">\n                        <span style=\"color: #828282;\">Simon Willison (quoting Luke Curley)</span> · May 9, 2026 · <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/May/9/webrtc/\">blog</a>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class=\"topics\">\n                        <span class=\"tag\">webrtc</span>\n                        <span class=\"tag\">openai</span>\n                        <span class=\"tag\">voice-ai</span>\n                        <span class=\"tag\">latency</span>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class=\"item\">\n                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n                        <span class=\"source-badge source-blog\">Blog</span>\n                        <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/May/8/html/\">Using Claude Code: The Unreasonable Effectiveness of HTML</a>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n                        Thariq Shihipar from Anthropic's Claude Code team advocates for HTML over Markdown as an output format for LLMs. The article demonstrates how requesting HTML output enables rich interactive explanations with SVG diagrams, interactive widgets, and in-page navigation.\n                    </div>\n                    <div class=\"item-meta\">\n                        <span style=\"color: #828282;\">Simon Willison</span> · May 8, 2026 · <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/May/8/html/\">blog</a>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class=\"topics\">\n                        <span class=\"tag\">claude-code</span>\n                        <span class=\"tag\">html</span>\n                        <span class=\"tag\">markdown</span>\n                        <span class=\"tag\">prompt-engineering</span>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n\n                <div class=\"item\">\n                    <div class=\"item-title\">\n                        <span class=\"source-badge source-blog\">Blog</span>\n                        <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/May/7/firefox-claude/\">Behind the Scenes Hardening Firefox with Claude Mythos Preview</a>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class=\"item-description\">\n                        Mozilla used early access to Claude Mythos to find and fix hundreds of vulnerabilities in Firefox. AI security research dramatically improved - from generating mostly unwanted slop just months ago to finding 423 security bugs in April 2026 (up from 20-30 per month through 2025).\n                    </div>\n                    <div class=\"item-meta\">\n                        <span style=\"color: #828282;\">Simon Willison</span> · May 7, 2026 · <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/May/7/firefox-claude/\">blog</a>\n                    </div>\n                    <div class=\"topics\">\n                        <span class=\"tag\">firefox</span>\n                        <span class=\"tag\">mozilla</span>\n                        <span class=\"tag\">security</span>\n                        <span class=\"tag\">ai</span>\n                        <span class=\"tag\">anthropic</span>\n                    </div>\n                </div>\n            </section>\n        </div>\n    </main>\n\n    <footer>\n        <div class=\"container\">\n            <a href=\"#\">top</a>\n            <a href=\"#new\">new</a>\n            <a href=\"#updates\">updates</a>\n            <a href=\"#insights\">insights</a>\n        </div>\n    </footer>\n</body>\n</html>"
}

